--- body ---
## Context

[[0378]] gives hunt a step 2b that triages a ticket before any code is
written: on a `Label: needs-human` tell it returns a batched decision list
instead of executing. That step is scoped, by 0378's own Verification bullet
3, to skip entirely for an already-detached executor — "a raid Phase 5
executor invoking `Skill(hunt)` proceeds straight to execution (no triage, no
recursive spawn)". The scoping is deliberate: the interactive hunt that
detaches has already run the triage, so re-running it in the executor buys
nothing and risks a recursive spawn.

It leaves one path unscreened. A raid picks its own tickets and spawns its own
Phase 5 executors, so no interactive hunt ever runs the triage for them.
pick-ticket is already covered: its step 1 calls `erg ready --json`, and every
label in `tickets/.ergrc` — `needs-human` included — suppresses a ticket from
`erg ready` output. But raid Phase 1 reads open tickets from `tickets/`
directly, never through `erg ready` or pick-ticket, and `skills/raid/SKILL.md`
never mentions `needs-human`. A `needs-human` ticket in an autonomous raid's
queue is therefore executed with no triage at all — the exact failure 0378
exists to prevent, at the exact altitude where the author is not watching.

The cost is measured, not hypothetical: both unmerged mixed kills of the
2026-07-28 climate-finance-het trace window (that project's 0334 and 0338)
burned full execution cycles before surfacing questions only the author could
settle. This clears the tooling-repo severity floor because it bites a science
repo.

The fix belongs upstream of hunt, not in step 2b: the screen should stop a
`needs-human` ticket from being *picked*, so no executor is spawned for it in
the first place. That is a cheaper place to stand than asking every executor to
re-triage, and it leaves 0378's recursion guard untouched.

## Relevant files

- `skills/pick-ticket/SKILL.md` — already screened via `erg ready`'s
  skip-label filter; at most, name that mechanism in the selection prose so
  the screen is visible where a reader looks for it.
- `skills/raid/SKILL.md` — Phase 1 target selection; must not hand a
  `needs-human` ticket to a Phase 5 executor.
- `skills/hunt/SKILL.md` — step 2b, unchanged by this ticket; its clause
  naming this upstream screen should stop pointing at an unfiled gap once
  this lands.
- `tickets/.ergrc` — `needs-human` is already in the default label set.

## Actions

1. Name `erg ready`'s skip-label filter (`tickets/.ergrc`) in pick-ticket's
   selection prose as the existing exclusion mechanism — do not add a second,
   hand-rolled label grep beside the tool-level filter that already does the
   job.
2. State in raid Phase 1 that a `needs-human` ticket is never a raid target,
   and say where such a ticket goes instead (surfaced to the author in the
   run report, as a success outcome — the same standing 0378 gives a returned
   decision list).
3. Decide and record whether an autonomous run that finds ONLY `needs-human`
   tickets reports an empty run or returns the decision lists.

## Test

- Red first: a skill-text guard asserting pick-ticket's selection section
  names `needs-human` as an exclusion, and a sibling asserting raid Phase 1
  does the same. Bounded-region extraction per
  `tests/test_hunt_triage_prestep.py`, not a file-wide substring match.

## Verification

- [ ] A ticket carrying `Label: needs-human` is not returned by pick-ticket
- [ ] A raid run whose queue contains a `needs-human` ticket does not spawn an
      executor for it and names it in the run report

## Invariants

- Hunt step 2b is unchanged (0378's Verification bullet 3 stands: a detached
  executor still skips the triage)
- The author can still force execution of a `needs-human` ticket explicitly

## Exit criteria

- [ ] pick-ticket names its existing skip-label screen and raid Phase 1 excludes `Label: needs-human`
- [ ] Guard tests in place and green
- [ ] `make check` and `make lint` pass
